

Kinetic‑Cryptographic Fluid Matrix (KCFM‑v1.0)

Cryptophysical Engine for Tamper‑Proof Hydraulic Telemetry and Real‑Time Infrastructure Integrity

Author: C. Matthew Blackwell

Overview

The Kinetic‑Cryptographic Fluid Matrix (KCFM‑v1.0) is a unified system architecture that fuses deterministic fluid dynamics, tensor‑based state representation, and entropy‑driven authenticated encryption into a single real‑time computational pipeline.

KCFM produces physically verified, cryptographically sealed hydraulic state records, enabling tamper‑proof telemetry, forensic reconstruction, and absolute infrastructure integrity.

Core Features

Deterministic Navier–Stokes + Darcy solver

Spatiotemporal tensor fieldization of hydraulic states

Kinetic‑entropy computation from velocity‑gradient fields

Entropy‑driven cryptographic key generation

AES‑GCM‑SIV authenticated encryption (optional lattice PQ layer)

Mass‑conservation invariant: ΔQ < 10⁻⁷

Cryptophysical ledger for immutable state storage

Zero‑latency compute‑to‑encrypt cycle

System Architecture

1. Physical Solver

Computes pressure, velocity components, turbulence scalars, and transient events using:

Navier–Stokes equations

Darcy flow relations

Time‑varying boundary conditions

Motor‑hammer transient modeling

2. State Tensorization

Fluid‑state variables are discretized into a tensor:

[ T_{i,j,k}(t) = [p, u, v, w, \eta]_{i,j,k}(t) ]

3. Vectorization

Tensor is flattened into a high‑dimensional state vector:

[ \mathbf{S}(t) = \text{flatten}(T(t)) ]

4. Kinetic‑Entropy Engine

Entropy is computed from velocity‑gradient intensity:

[ H_{\text{kinetic}} = \int |\nabla \mathbf{u}|^2 dV ]

5. Key Generation

Cryptographic key derived via HKDF:

[ K(t) = \text{HKDF}(H_{\text{kinetic}} \parallel \mathbf{S}(t)) ]

6. Authenticated Encryption

State vector encrypted using AES‑GCM‑SIV:

[ C(t) = \text{Encrypt}(\mathbf{S}(t), K(t)) ]

7. Verification Layer

Mass conservation:[ |\Delta Q| < 10^{-7} ]

Integrity hashes: CRC32 + SHA‑3

Replay protection: entropy‑bound key uniqueness

8. Cryptophysical Ledger

Append‑only storage of:

Ciphertext

Mass‑conservation values

Integrity hashes

Timestamps

Entropy signatures

